---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import figMaillard from '../../../assets/img/fig-maillard-temperature.webp';
---

<ArticleLayout slug="maillard-reactions">
  <p>
    Proteins and sugars react together to form complexes that are
    indigestible in the rumen: bypass proteins. These Maillard compounds are
    dissolved thanks to the acid pH of the abomasum; the amino acids they
    contain therefore become fully available again for absorption in the
    intestine.
  </p>
  <p>
    Maillard reactions occur in the presence of amino acids and sugars at
    temperatures between 0 and 150&nbsp;°C, a pH between 6 and 10 and a
    moisture level of around 15%. As the graph below shows, temperature
    has a strong influence on the speed of the reaction.
  </p>
  <!-- TODO: contenu client - cette figure porte du texte français
       incrusté dans l'image : faire produire une version anglaise.
       L'alt et la légende sont traduits, pas le visuel. -->
  <figure>
    <Image
      src={figMaillard}
      alt="Graph: influence of temperature on the speed of the Maillard reaction"
      widths={[600, 900]}
      width={900}
      quality={70}
      sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 700px"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Influence of temperature on the Maillard reaction.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    During extrusion cooking, it is therefore possible to adjust the
    parameters - temperature and residence time of the mix - in order to
    trigger Maillard reactions or not. It is worth encouraging these
    reactions for the production of ruminant feed, but it will be important
    to limit them as far as possible for the production of poultry feed, for
    example: Maillard compounds reduce protein availability for monogastric
    species.
  </p>
  <p>
    Good control of extrusion cooking therefore makes it possible to vary the
    characteristics of the product according to the anatomy of the target
    species.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
